<binaryimage arch="ARM:LE:32:v7">
<!--
    GH-781: an exact ARM veneer bound turns a valid tail transfer into
    halt_missing.

    A linker veneer loads its target from a literal and jumps through a
    register:

        0x1000 veneer:     ldr r1,[pc,#0]   ; r1 = *(0x1008) = 0x1010
        0x1004             bx  r1
        0x1008             .word 0x1010
        0x1010 arm_helper: add r0,r0,#1
        0x1014             bx  lr

    Jump-table recovery reads the read-only literal and yields a one-entry
    table whose only target is arm_helper's entry.  The follower walked INTO
    arm_helper as a single-case switch; with the veneer's exact extent
    declared (`function bounds 0x1000 0x1008`) the target lies outside it,
    so the case became halt_missing() under a "flows out of bounds" warning.

    Stage / sub-stage: P2 "flow-classification".  The P0 assertion is
    `option tailcalljump on|direct`: under `on` such a jump is a tail call
    when the callee's prototype is locked or the destination is outside the
    declared extent; `direct` keeps the flow into the destination.

    The image also holds an `ldr pc,[pc,#-4]` veneer to 0x1020, which is not
    a function (out of bounds in both passes); a Thumb veneer at 0x1028 to
    thumb_helper, where neither side has a prototype but the helper is
    outside the extent (a tail call whose target register r1 = 0x1031 is not
    an argument); an
    unbounded veneer at 0x1038 to arm_helper (a tail call because the
    callee's prototype is declared); and an unbounded veneer at 0x1048 to
    plain_helper, which has no prototype, so it keeps the copied-in body
    that states its return value in both passes.  bare_veneer at 0x1060 is
    the bounded A32 form with no prototype on either side: a tail call whose
    target register r1 = plain_helper is not an argument.  ret_veneer at
    0x1070 is the unbounded veneer to arm_helper with no prototype of its
    own: a call would drop the value arm_helper returns, since kuna's own
    return recovery never takes a tail call's result, so it keeps the
    copied-in body in both passes.

    Pass 1 (option tailcalljump direct) reproduces the bug: #1, #4.
    Pass 2 (on): #2, #3 and #6 are the tail calls; #5, #7 and #9 hold in
    both.
-->
